>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="law">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do Wirral landlords need a CP12?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. If you rent out a home with gas on the Wirral, you need a CP12 every 12 months. The check must be done by a Gas Safe registered engineer.</p>
      <p>The rules come from the <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/landlords/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998</a>. They apply to every rented home, from a flat in Birkenhead to a house in Heswall.</p>
      <h3>What you must do</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Get a new gas safety check every 12 months.</li>
        <li>Give your tenants a copy within 28 days of the check.</li>
        <li>Give new tenants a copy before they move in.</li>
        <li>Keep each record for at least 2 years.</li>
        <li>Fit a carbon monoxide alarm in any room with a gas boiler or other fixed fuel-burning appliance.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>HMOs on the Wirral</h3>
      <p>Many HMOs are in Birkenhead CH41, Wallasey CH44 and Bebington CH63. A Wirral Council HMO licence needs a valid gas safety certificate, renewed each year. HMOs often have a shared gas hob as well as the boiler. We check them all in one visit.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Changing tenants?</strong> This is the date people miss most. If the old CP12 will run out during the new tenancy, renew it first. Your new tenant must get a valid copy before they move in.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="gas-fires">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Got a gas fire or back boiler?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Tell us when you book. If you supply a gas fire, it must be on the certificate. We do not check gas fires, but we can disconnect and cap one safely.</p>
      <p>Many older terraces in Birkenhead, Rock Ferry, Tranmere and Wallasey still have a gas fire in the front room. Some have an old back boiler behind it. A CP12 that lists the boiler but not your gas fire is not complete.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Do not need the fire?</h3>
          <p>We disconnect and cap it safely. It then no longer needs to be on the certificate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Want to keep it?</h3>
          <p>It must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires. We check the boiler, cooker and hob.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Still have a back boiler? We can take it out, cap the fire and fit a new combi. See <a href="/boiler-installation-wirral">boiler installation on the Wirral</a>.</p>
      <p>The same rule covers cookers and hobs. If it was there when the tenant moved in, it is yours to certify. If the tenant brought it, it is theirs.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="checks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What does a CP12 check?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We check every gas appliance you supply, the pipework and the air supply. The aim is to make sure nothing can leak gas or carbon monoxide.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The gas supply</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>A tightness test to prove there are no leaks</li>
            <li>Gas pressure at the meter and each appliance</li>
            <li>The emergency control valve works</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Each appliance</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>It burns gas safely, with a flue gas test on the boiler</li>
            <li>The flue takes fumes outside</li>
            <li>There is enough fresh air</li>
            <li>Safety devices cut the gas off when they should</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Air vents matter in older Birkenhead and Wallasey terraces. They are often blocked or painted over. We check them and test the carbon monoxide alarm too.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does it work?</h2>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call or book online.</strong> Tell us the address and every gas appliance, including any gas fire. We give you a fixed price.</li>
        <li><strong>We arrange access.</strong> We agree a time with you, your tenant or your agent. You do not need to be on the Wirral.</li>
        <li><strong>The check.</strong> A Gas Safe registered engineer checks everything. Most homes take under an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Your certificate.</strong> We email the CP12 the same day, ready to send to your tenant.</li>
        <li><strong>Next year.</strong> We remind you before it runs out, so you never miss a date.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need a CP12 on the Wirral this week?</strong> Call and we will find the next free slot.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="fail">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What happens if something fails?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We tell you straight away and explain what it means. Gas engineers use three labels, set by the gas industry.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk stk-note">
          <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What it means</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>Immediately dangerous</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">It is a risk to life right now.</td><td data-label="What happens">With your consent, we disconnect it. It must not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>At risk</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">A fault could become dangerous.</td><td data-label="What happens">With your consent, we turn it off. It should not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Label"><strong>Not to current standards</strong></td><td data-label="What it means">It met older rules, but not today's.</td><td data-label="What happens">It can stay in use. We note it on the certificate.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If a repair is needed, we give you the price first. Nothing starts without your say-so. Once everything is safe, we issue the certificate.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="wirral">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Where on the Wirral do you do CP12s?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Everywhere on the peninsula. The price is the same in every CH postcode.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Birkenhead and central Wirral</h3>
          <p>Birkenhead CH41, Rock Ferry and Tranmere CH42, Prenton CH43. Lots of older terraces, many with gas fires.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>North Wirral</h3>
          <p>Wallasey CH44, New Brighton CH45, Moreton and Leasowe CH46. Many rented homes and a mix of house ages.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>South Wirral</h3>
          <p>Heswall CH60, Bromborough and Eastham CH62, Bebington CH63, Neston CH64.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Buying or selling on the Wirral</h3>
      <p>A CP12 is not only for landlords. Sellers can use one to answer a buyer's questions early. Buyers can book one to check the boiler, cooker and hob before they move in.</p>
      <div class="brx-note">
        <p><strong>Landlords also need an EICR.</strong> A gas safety certificate covers the gas. The electrics need an EICR at least every 5 years. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/eicr-wirral">Ideal Electrical Solutions does landlord EICRs on the Wirral</a> from £149 including VAT, NICEIC approved, with the certificate emailed within 24 hours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
